33. Perceived stress (PSS)-[POSITIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'session_id': '7290e838-860e-4375-ab04-d90e495db40b', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8134648598222611, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/1395490', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/npp201690', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC2904148/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10493032810336607, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that perceived stress directly stimulates cytokine production in peripheral tissues as a psychological coping mechanism, independent of central nervous system involvement. However, the logic is flawed: perceived stress is an internal psychological state, and there is no clear causal pathway described for how this perception, in the absence of central nervous system mediation, could directly affect peripheral tissue cytokine production. The rationale that increasing cytokines serves as a local coping mechanism for stress is not logically substantiated within the explanation. The reasoning overlooks established pathways (e.g., neuroendocrine signaling) and instead proposes a direct effect without logical justification.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.8548342877153974, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6554928911792648, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes an increase in proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by directly stimulating the production of these cytokines within peripheral tissues such as muscle and adipose cells, independent of central nervous system involvement. Individuals who experience higher perceived stress are thought to generate more TNF-alpha and IL-6 locally as a psychological coping mechanism to promote tissue repair and adaptation to environmental challenges. Therefore, lower perceived stress is associated with decreased peripheral cytokine production due to reduced need for tissue-level adaptive responses.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes an increase in proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by directly stimulating the production of these cytokines within peripheral tissues such as muscle and adipose cells, independent of central nervous system involvement. Individuals who experience higher perceived stress are thought to generate more TNF-alpha and IL-6 locally as a psychological coping mechanism to promote tissue repair and adaptation to environmental challenges. Therefore, lower perceived stress is associated with decreased peripheral cytokine production due to reduced need for tissue-level adaptive responses.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.004945774296149133, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8864947781826717, 'min_prob': 0.0959484175066183, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7092064800650814, 'token_prob_slope': -0.001891842586753709, 'prob_variance': 0.05774849521479304, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0006611426484557965, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3239295110102907, 'avg_nll': 0.20109990044264467, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.4107727480299068, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through direct neuroendocrine and immune mechanisms. Higher perceived stress activates the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis and sympathetic nervous system, which in turn promotes the production of proinflammatory cytokines such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces activation of these inflammatory pathways.', 'prob_std': 0.24030916589841728, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.2227469186803817, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8146075563613779}

34. Perceived stress (PSS)-[POSITIVE]->Smoking (SMQ)
   Properties: {'session_id': '7290e838-860e-4375-ab04-d90e495db40b', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5877822037978917, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3776130/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4855524/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-017-07579-w'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09592803831599356, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation claims that higher perceived stress causes slower nicotine metabolism, which in turn increases physiological dependence and thus smoking frequency. However, this reasoning is problematic. It assumes a direct biological pathway from psychological stress to altered nicotine metabolism, which is not logically established in the explanation. Additionally, if nicotine remains longer in the system due to slower metabolism, this would generally reduce the need for more frequent smoking, not increase it. Thus, the explanation contains internal logical inconsistencies and does not coherently support the positive relationship claimed.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7427233602404943, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6698530782593526, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to an increase in smoking (SMQ) because individuals who report higher levels of stress are biologically predisposed to metabolize nicotine at a slower rate, causing nicotine to remain in their system longer. As a result, they develop a greater physiological dependence, which subsequently increases their frequency of smoking. This direct biological pathway explains the positive association between perceived stress and smoking behavior.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to an increase in smoking (SMQ) because individuals who report higher levels of stress are biologically predisposed to metabolize nicotine at a slower rate, causing nicotine to remain in their system longer. As a result, they develop a greater physiological dependence, which subsequently increases their frequency of smoking. This direct biological pathway explains the positive association between perceived stress and smoking behavior.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.04643442998544198, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8035056880216991, 'min_prob': 0.07236537457923119, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7408242940709825, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005071911696602454, 'prob_variance': 0.0733691295429888, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 1.866165131754913e-05, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30972251825786506, 'avg_nll': 0.3270694634007274, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1391167779200277, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes smoking (SMQ) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can lead individuals to increase smoking as a coping mechanism, while lower stress tends to reduce the urge to smoke. This effect is widely observed in behavioral health research and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'prob_std': 0.2708673652232561, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3868978124728906, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0635068926192273}

35. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2868753366380121, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) in a direct causal relationship. Higher perceived stress increases the likelihood and severity of depressive symptoms, while lower perceived stress reduces them. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where chronic stress directly impacts mood, cognition, and neurobiology, leading to depression. The effect is direct and not fully mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '7290e838-860e-4375-ab04-d90e495db40b', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6616193427474304, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09586149324987736, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation posits that higher perceived stress leads to reduced depressive symptoms because it triggers adaptive coping mechanisms. However, this reasoning assumes that greater stress reliably results in improved coping and emotional management, which is not logically necessary; stress can also overwhelm coping abilities and increase depressive symptoms. The causal chain proposed is plausible in some cases (e.g., stress inoculation), but the explanation ignores scenarios where increased stress impairs, rather than enhances, coping. Thus, the reasoning is incomplete and oversimplified.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6693698134907508, 'motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress are thought to trigger adaptive coping mechanisms, which in turn can lead to a reduction in depressive symptoms. Therefore, as individuals experience more stress, they become better at managing their emotions, resulting in lower scores on measures of depression.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress are thought to trigger adaptive coping mechanisms, which in turn can lead to a reduction in depressive symptoms. Therefore, as individuals experience more stress, they become better at managing their emotions, resulting in lower scores on measures of depression.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.010981630540201494, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.734812147746299, 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.0010663931624653531, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30961507271106387}

36. Perceived stress (PSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'session_id': '7290e838-860e-4375-ab04-d90e495db40b', 'judge_perplexity': 1.5701522525695546, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121000192', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.08339583713708906, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation logically connects perceived stress to reduced anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) via behavioral changes (e.g., less healthy eating and exercise). It claims that these behaviors are the primary drivers of IL-10 levels, and thus, interventions should focus on health behaviors rather than direct physiological responses to stress. However, the reasoning overlooks the possibility that stress could also directly affect IL-10 through physiological pathways (e.g., neuroendocrine or immune system changes), not just indirectly via behavior. By asserting that lifestyle choices are the primary drivers, the explanation overstates the case and neglects plausible direct effects, making the causal reasoning incomplete.\\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_CORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7864973595995834, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6321123583249547, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because individuals experiencing high levels of stress are less likely to engage in health-promoting behaviors such as consuming anti-inflammatory foods or maintaining regular exercise routines. This behavioral avoidance directly diminishes the production of IL-10, as lifestyle choices are the primary drivers of anti-inflammatory cytokine activity. Therefore, interventions targeting perceived stress (PSS) are expected to enhance anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) chiefly by improving health behaviors rather than through direct physiological stress responses.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because individuals experiencing high levels of stress are less likely to engage in health-promoting behaviors such as consuming anti-inflammatory foods or maintaining regular exercise routines. This behavioral avoidance directly diminishes the production of IL-10, as lifestyle choices are the primary drivers of anti-inflammatory cytokine activity. Therefore, interventions targeting perceived stress (PSS) are expected to enhance anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) chiefly by improving health behaviors rather than through direct physiological stress responses.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.10202929916407882, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173503177894934, 'min_prob': 0.06566455284138531, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7267020783400263, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003752638027729859, 'prob_variance': 0.08607163795447598, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.0008000202482546566, 'judge_prob_std': 0.28878337406625243, 'avg_nll': 0.3415744130364601, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2443118764649557, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease through physiological stress pathways. Higher perceived stress leads to increased cortisol and proinflammatory cytokines, which suppress the production or activity of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10. Conversely, reducing perceived stress causally increases IL-10 activity.', 'prob_std': 0.29337968224550925, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.407161300555381, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8248579307602297}

