   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2821081561305312, 'session_id': '7290e838-860e-4375-ab04-d90e495db40b', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7403126529892141, 'prob_std': 0.2598500225601379, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10957925712042713, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8957598/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-85514-0', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0149763422000094'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7977772921290683, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation logically connects rumination to proinflammatory processes by describing how sustained psychological stress from rumination could activate physiological stress pathways, thereby elevating inflammatory biomarkers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. The explanation is coherent and the causal chain is internally logical, with each step following plausibly from the previous one. The statement that reducing rumination would decrease these processes is a reasonable inverse of the proposed mechanism. Although it cites external research, the internal logic suffices for a sound causal explanation.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6716845671459426, 'motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through sustained psychological stress, which activates physiological stress pathways and elevates inflammatory biomarkers. Increased rumination prolongs stress responses, leading to higher levels of TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing rumination would decrease these proinflammatory processes. This causal link is supported by psychoneuroimmunology research on stress and inflammation.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.01673758681633825, 'perplexity': 1.3238634965689235, 'mean_token_prob': 0.843