icial light exposure and restore healthy sleep patterns.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals experiencing heightened loneliness tend to spend more time indoors, which directly exposes them to increased artificial light at night. This excessive exposure disrupts circadian rhythms by suppressing melatonin production, resulting in poorer sleep quality as measured by the PSQI. Therefore, interventions aimed at reducing loneliness would naturally decrease artificial light exposure and restore healthy sleep patterns.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0042812457951963276, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7925160192210917, 'min_prob': 0.01345799719428585, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7177635664571644, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006372715502487559, 'prob_variance': 0.08053359200908575, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.0002321442795303004, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3175164372461384, 'avg_nll': 0.3641636348242396, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1220055571843799, 'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased feelings of loneliness can heighten psychological stress and arousal, making it harder to fall and stay asleep. Conversely, reducing loneliness can lead to fewer sleep disturbances. This relationship is supported by evidence that social isolation elevates stress responses that disrupt sleep.', 'prob_std': 0.2837844111453019, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4393097160817803, 'max_window_entropy': 1.1018933899817307}

