   Properties: {'session_id': '7290e838-860e-4375-ab04-d90e495db40b', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8741518864610247, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11010940162764137, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation lays out a plausible mechanism for a negative relationship between prosocial behavior (PGG) and perceived stress (PSS) by positing that engaging in prosocial acts diverts cognitive attention from one\'s own stressors to those of others, thereby reducing activation in stress-related brain regions and lowering perceived stress. The logical sequence is internally consistent: increased prosocial behavior leads to less focus on personal stress, resulting in less perceived stress. Similarly, decreased prosocial behavior is said to increase attention to personal stressors, raising perceived stress. While the explanation is coherent and makes logical sense, it assumes that cognitive resources are zero-sum and that brain regions involved in stress processing are directly deactivated by prosocial focus, without addressing potential confounds or alternative pathways. However, within the scope of the internal logic, the causal reasoning is sound.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7340362605266764, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6379678543949959, 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Frequent engagement in prosocial behavior (PGG) directly reduces perceived s