 if their external social environment remains unchanged.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to reductions in loneliness (UCLS) because the act of helping others causes a neurochemical response that directly diminishes the subjective experience of loneliness, regardless of actual changes in social interaction or social networks. Thus, individuals who engage in more prosocial behavior (PGG) experience lower loneliness (UCLS) as a result of these internal biochemical changes, even if their external social environment remains unchanged.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.023221339199239927, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8499054709941883, 'min_prob': 0.20929682931747493, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6908400834010967, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004251045182859988, 'prob_variance': 0.047677240803928565, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.0003724674748662833, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30784946582866446, 'avg_nll': 0.21243737065657642, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.4381742738730405, 'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes loneliness (UCLS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in more prosocial behavior directly reduces feelings of loneliness by increasing social connection and positive social interactions. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior can directly increase loneliness due to reduced opportunities for meaningful social engagement.', 'prob_std': 0.21835118686173557, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.23668865814114, 'max_window_entropy': 0.8306519224414681}

