19. Daily hassles (HS)-[NEGATIVE]->Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2508489572699548, 'original_motivation': 'Daily hassles (HS) do not directly cause anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to change. Any effect of daily hassles on IL-10 is likely mediated by other variables such as perceived stress or proinflammatory processes already present in the CLD. There is no established direct causal link from daily hassles to anti-inflammatory processes.', 'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7736810368452012, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10518567024809891, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation posits that frequent daily hassles (source variable) can suppress anti-inflammatory processes, specifically the production of IL-10 (target variable), via stress. The chain of reasoning is: daily hassles \\u2192 constant stress \\u2192 direct inhibition of anti-inflammatory cytokine (IL-10) production \\u2192 increased susceptibility to inflammation. This is a coherent causal chain: it identifies a plausible mediator (stress), describes a mechanism (inhibition of cytokine production), and links this to the outcome (less anti-inflammatory response). No logical errors or unjustified leaps are present within the explanation itself; all steps are connected and scientifically plausible in their phrasing.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6411968529250736, 'motivation': "Frequent exposure to daily hassles is thought to suppress anti-inflammatory processes, such as the production of IL-10. This is because the constant stress from daily irritations could directly inhibit the body's ability to produce anti-inflammatory cytokines, making individuals more susceptible to inflammation.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_v