   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.411819712324407, 'original_motivation': 'Daily hassles (HS) do not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Daily hassles (HS) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking the experience of daily hassles with the concept of spatial scale. Without a defined pathway or mechanism, no causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 9.756072229983208e+16, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10866939436170969, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation proposes that an increase in daily hassles (source variable) causes individuals to perceive their environments as larger or more spread out (target variable: spatial scale). The stated mechanism is that stress from daily hassles makes distances and spaces feel more overwhelming, which translates into perceiving spatial scale as larger. Logically, the chain of reasoning is as follows: more hassles \\u2192 more stress \\u2192 environments feel more overwhelming \\u2192 perception of greater spatial scale."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5717278211331246, 'motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles causes individuals to perceive their environments as larger or more spread out, because stress makes distances and spaces feel more overwhelming. This leads to a direct positive relationship between the frequency of daily hassles and the perceived spatial scale.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Experiencing more daily hassles causes individuals to perceive their environments as larger or more spread out, because stress makes distances and spaces feel more overwhelming. This leads to 