   Properties: {'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7160825198543157, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4558262/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3776130/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4855524/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-017-07579-w'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.0929787954268505, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that perceived stress leads to increased smoking because stressed individuals metabolize nicotine more slowly.  \\nStep 2: It then claims that slower nicotine metabolism causes nicotine to remain in the body longer, leading to greater physiological dependence.  \\nStep 3: The argument concludes that this greater dependence causes increased frequency of smoking, thus explaining the positive association.  \\nStep 4: Assessing logical coherence: The explanation hinges on the assumption that higher stress causes slower nicotine metabolism, which in turn increases dependence and thus smoking.  \\nStep 5: However, if nicotine remains in the system longer, it could plausibly reduce the need to smoke as frequently (since the pharmacological effects last longer), rather than increase it. The chain from slower metabolism to higher dependence to more frequent smoking is not logically robust.  \\nStep 6: Furthermore, the explanation does not address psychological mechanisms (such as stress-induced craving or coping) that would more directly link stress to increased smoking, instead focusing on a biological mechanism that seems internally inconsistent.  \\nStep 7: Overall, the reasoning contains a logical flaw in the direction of the mechanism and lacks coherence in connecting the proposed biological pathway to increased smoking."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7427233602404943, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6036069420043799, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to an increase in smoking (SMQ) because individuals who report higher levels of stress are biologically predisposed to metabolize nicotine at a slower rate, causing nicotine to remain in their system longer. As a result, they develop a greater physiological dependence, which subsequently increases their frequency of smoking. This direct biological pathway explains the positive association between perceived stress and smoking behavior.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to an increase in smoking (SMQ) because individuals who report higher levels of stress are biologically predisposed to metabolize nicotine at a slower rate, causing nicotine to remain in their system longer. As a result, they develop a greater physiological dependence, which subsequently increases their frequency of smoking. This direct biological pathway explains the positive association between perceived stress and smoking behavior.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.004171723967536269, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8035056880216991, 'min_prob': 0.07236537457923119, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6968405433937452, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005071911696602454, 'prob_variance': 0.0733691295429888, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0006918629187873434, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3049242453903108, 'avg_nll': 0.3270694634007274, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3112225679237732, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes smoking (SMQ) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress can lead individuals to increase smoking as a coping mechanism, while lower stress tends to reduce the urge to smoke. This effect is widely observed in behavioral health research and does not require mediation by other variables in the provided list.', 'prob_std': 0.2708673652232561, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3868978124728906, 'max_window_entropy': 1.0635068926192273}

