   Properties: {'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 909069534160445.5, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4065693/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159103000291', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0889159121000192', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41398-022-01921-1'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10260876513974525, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation posits that perceived stress (PSS) reduces anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) via a behavioral pathway: stressed individuals are less likely to engage in health-promoting behaviors (diet, exercise), which in turn lowers IL-10.  \\nStep 2: The explanation further states that lifestyle choices are the primary drivers of IL-10 activity and downplays or excludes a direct physiological pathway from stress to IL-10.  \\nStep 3: The internal logic is generally coherent: high stress \\u2192 reduced healthy behaviors \\u2192 lower IL-10. This creates a plausible mediational path from PSS to IL-10.  \\nStep 4: However, the claim that lifestyle choices are the primary drivers of anti-inflammatory cytokine activity is overly strong and not necessarily justified by the premises presented. The explanation omits the possibility of a direct physiological effect of stress on IL-10, which weakens the completeness and accuracy of the causal reasoning within the explanation itself.  \\nStep 5: The conclusion that interventions targeting PSS would enhance IL-10 chiefly by improving health behaviors assumes without justification that the behavioral route is dominant, rather than equally or less important than direct physiological effects."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7864973595995834, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6470076703614108, 'motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because individuals experiencing high levels of stress are less likely to engage in health-promoting behaviors such as consuming anti-inflammatory foods or maintaining regular exercise routines. This behavioral avoidance directly diminishes the production of IL-10, as lifestyle choices are the primary drivers of anti-inflammatory cytokine activity. Therefore, interventions targeting perceived stress (PSS) are expected to enhance anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) chiefly by improving health behaviors rather than through direct physiological stress responses.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) leads to a reduction in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) because individuals experiencing high levels of stress are less likely to engage in health-promoting behaviors such as consuming anti-inflammatory foods or maintaining regular exercise routines. This behavioral avoidance directly diminishes the production of IL-10, as lifestyle choices are the primary drivers of anti-inflammatory cytokine activity. Therefore, interventions targeting perceived stress (PSS) are expected to enhance anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) chiefly by improving health behaviors rather than through direct physiological stress responses.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8173503177894934, 'min_prob': 0.06566455284138531, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7144082631366875, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003752638027729859, 'prob_variance': 0.08607163795447598, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0006515993408226329, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3203260294446039, 'avg_nll': 0.3415744130364601, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.483580831638468, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to decrease through physiological stress pathways. Higher per