   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2354741058004728, 'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7327497217952592, 'prob_std': 0.3006648740106923, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10311962700195491, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3897879/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10253890.2022.2054697'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7577963906041068, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation clearly states that there is no direct causal relationship between perceived stress (PSS) and prosocial behavior (PGG). It acknowledges the existence of possible indirect or mediated pathways but asserts that, in the absence of specified direct mechanisms, no direct link should be drawn. The reasoning is internally consistent: it avoids over-claiming causality and aligns the structure of the CLD (Causal Loop Diagram) with the absence of evidence for direct causation. The logic follows that without direct mechanisms, a direct link would be unwarranted. The explanation is careful and methodical, making the causal reasoning sound within its stated context."}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.626806340478187, 'motivation': 'There is no established direct causal relationship where perceived stress (PSS) causes prosocial behavior (PGG) or vice versa. Mechanisms linking perceived stress to prosocial actions are either indirect or mediated by psychological and social factors, none of which are specified as direct pathways in this context. Therefore, no direct causal link should be included in the CLD.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'j