73. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Perceived stress (PSS)
   Properties: {'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7908146011220198, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://www.annualreviews.org/content/journals/10.1146/annurev.so.18.080192.000311', 'https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/abs/10.1086/514478', 'https://cdc.gov/dhdsp/health_equity/psychosocial.htm'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.103572783742127, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation begins by stating that perceived social support leads to reduced perceived stress (implying social support is the cause and reduced stress is the effect). However, the main argument reverses this direction: it claims that lower stress levels make individuals more approachable, allowing them to attract more social support. This means that the explanation is actually describing a scenario where reduced stress leads to increased perceived social support, not the other way around. Thus, the causal direction presented in the explanation does not align with the original relationship type specified (that social support causes reduced stress). The reasoning is internally consistent but does not match the required direction of causality."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7694620165899766, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6388444690982645, 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) leads to a reduction in perceived stress (PSS) primarily because individuals who experience lower levels of stress naturally attract more supportive social networks. As stress levels decline, people become more approachable and socially engaged, which in turn increases their access to emotional and instrumental support. Thus, the observed negative relationship arises because decreased perceived stress (PSS) enables higher perceived social support (MSPSS), rather than social support being the antecedent of reduced stress.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) leads to a reduction in perceived stress (PSS) primarily because individuals who experience lower levels of stress naturally attract more supportive social networks. As stress levels decline, people become more approachable and socially engaged, which in turn increases their access to emotional and instrumental support. Thus, the observed negative relationship arises because decreased perceived stress (PSS) enables higher perceived social support (MSPSS), rather than social support being the antecedent of reduced stress.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.009294230795714576, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8344091017553108, 'min_prob': 0.04643930140651386, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7012941905619885, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005745404398645487, 'prob_variance': 0.06658089569030737, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00010968927670685838, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3218272576121031, 'avg_nll': 0.27523663904165846, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2713481113504528, 'original_motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Higher perceived social support directly reduces perceived stress by providing emotional and practical resources to cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived social support increases perceived stress due to fewer coping resources. This causal pathway is well-established in the psychological literature.', 'prob_std': 0.2580327415083353, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.3168422542893101, 'max_window_entropy': 1.1903497644018755}

