works, they may develop a greater reliance on external validation, which can undermine their sense of personal agency and self-sufficiency. As a result, even minor fluctuations or perceived inadequacies in support can heighten feelings of isolation and loneliness, suggesting that increased Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally leads to higher Loneliness (UCLS) through a process of dependency and unmet expectations.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of Perceived social support (MSPSS) can paradoxically contribute to increased Loneliness (UCLS) over time. When individuals consistently receive support from their social networks, they may develop a greater reliance on external validation, which can undermine their sense of personal agency and self-sufficiency. As a result, even minor fluctuations or perceived inadequacies in support can heighten feelings of isolation and loneliness, suggesting that increased Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally leads to higher Loneliness (UCLS) through a process of dependency and unmet expectations.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.027904253283753323, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8636851851297134, 'min_prob': 0.09082121621548855, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7140414150262975, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003614465071846423, 'prob_variance': 0.052530277347119556, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': 0.00017844768497803646, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3254379185657345, 'avg_nll': 0.21364801332071406, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2831391723352217, 'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased loneliness directly leads to decreased perceived social support, as individuals who feel lonelier tend to perceive less support from others. Conversely, decreased loneliness is causally linked to increased perceived social support.', 'prob_std': 0.22919484581272667, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious