   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.1733798506514324, 'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.693815237725196, 'prob_std': 0.28815809084271543, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10227043125851107, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9910279/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167494319300433', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30825769/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35087386/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23630220/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8014467154346091, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation logically links loneliness to increased proinflammatory processes by referencing activation of stress-related biological pathways. It reasonably argues that social isolation and perceived loneliness can trigger physiological stress responses, which could, in turn, elevate inflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. The step connecting loneliness to activation of stress pathways is plausible, and the subsequent step from stress activation to increased inflammation is also logical. However, the explanation asserts this is a \\"direct positive causal relationship\\" and states that \\"reducing loneliness directly decreases these proinflammatory processes,\\" which oversimplifies the causality. The reasoning does not sufficiently address possible mediators or confounding variables, and it conflates correlation and direct causation. Thus, while the chain of reasoning is generally plausible, it overstates the directness and exclusivity of the relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6578054350042276, 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) to increase through activation of stress-related biological pathways. Social isolation and perceived loneliness trigger physiological stress responses, elevating inflammatory markers such as TNF-alpha and IL-6. Conversely, reducing loneliness directly decreases these proinflammatory processes. This is a direct positive causal relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.016020443154598007, 'perplexity': 1.357219736374002, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8333302365868627, 'min_prob': 0.08278921643946109, 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7326850568271837, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0033918450006394114, 'prob_variance': 0.08303508531811865, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9080288535825858, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00016065362469284462, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3197974847595132, 'avg_nll': 0.3054382958012804}

