   Properties: {'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 508871038099016.0, 'citations': ['https://iaap-journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1111/aphw.12586', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10900038/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4225959/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-93681-3'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10830327275511699, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation claims that loneliness leads to increased time indoors.  \\nStep 2: It asserts that more time indoors results in increased exposure to artificial light at night.  \\nStep 3: It further claims that this increased exposure disrupts circadian rhythms via melatonin suppression, leading to poorer sleep quality.  \\nStep 4: Finally, it concludes that reducing loneliness will decrease artificial light exposure and improve sleep."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7208186397356824, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5716675784774654, 'motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals experiencing heightened loneliness tend to spend more time indoors, which directly exposes them to increased artificial light at night. This excessive exposure disrupts circadian rhythms by suppressing melatonin production, resulting in poorer sleep quality as measured by the PSQI. Therefore, interventions aimed at reducing loneliness would naturally decrease artificial light exposure and restore healthy sleep patterns.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'spurious_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) because individuals experiencing heightened loneliness tend to spend more time indoors, which directly exposes them to increased artificial light at night. This excessive exposure disrupts circadian rhythms by suppressing melatonin production, resulting in poorer sleep quality as measured by the PSQI. Therefore, interventions aimed at reducing loneliness would naturally decrease artificial light exposure and restore healthy sleep patterns.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7925160192210917, 'min_prob': 0.01345799719428585, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6550138038083266, 'token_prob_slope': -0.006372715502487559, 'prob_variance': 0.08053359200908575, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.000814627357134825, 'judge_prob_std': 0.32909462583748916, 'avg_nll': 0.3641636348242396, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3668343544189832, 'original_motivation': 'Loneliness (UCLS) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) in a direct causal relationship. Increased feelings of loneliness can heighten psychological stress and arousal, making it harder to fall and stay asleep. Conversely, reducing loneliness can lead to fewer sleep disturbances. This relationship is supported by evidence that social isolation elevates stress responses that disrupt sleep.', 'prob_std': 0.2837844111453019, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.4393097160817803, 'max_window_entropy': 1.1018933899817307}

99. Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR)-[NEGATIVE]->Sleep disturbance (PSQI)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2588269641545173, 'original_motivation': 'Depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) through well-established psychological and physiological mechanisms, such as increased rumination, negative affect, and disruptions in circadian rhythm. An increase in depressive symptoms directly leads to an increase in sleep disturbance, while a decrease in depressive symptoms directly reduces sleep disturbance. This effect is not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list. The causal direction is from depressive symptoms to sleep disturbance.', 'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9249111574704925, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11625890087474432, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation proposes that increased depressive symptoms lead to more fatigue, and this greater fatigue causes people to fall asleep more easily, thus reducing sleep disturbances and improving sleep quality. Step by step, the chain of reasoning is: (1) Depressive symptoms cause increased fatigue; (2) Increased fatigue makes it easier to fall asleep; (3) Easier sleep onset leads to fewer sleep disturbances and better sleep quality. However, this logic contains a questionable leap: while fatigue can sometimes make one feel sleepy, depressive symptoms are also associated with factors like rumination, anxiety, and altered sleep architecture, which can increase sleep disturbances. The assumption that higher depressive symptoms would generally lead to better sleep quality due to fatigue oversimplifies the causal chain and ignores the possibility that depression-related fatigue does not always translate to improved sleep, and may, in fact, coexist with poor sleep. Thus, the explanation\'s internal logic is weak and does not adequately account for possible counteracting mechanisms within the same source variable.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6221148520501729, 'motivation': 'It is possible that as depressive symptoms increase, individuals become more fatigued and thus experience fewer sleep disturbances, falling asleep more easily due to exhaustion. Therefore, higher depressive symptoms could actually lead to better sleep quality and reduced sleep disturbance.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is possible that as depressive symptoms increase, individuals become more fatigued and thus experience fewer sleep disturbances, falling asleep more easily due to exhaustion. Therefore, higher depressive symptoms could actually lead to better sleep quality and reduced sleep disturbance.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.01575697484945578, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6734906536622707, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0003720414756365616, 'judge_prob_std': 0.34096759505082636}

