   Properties: {'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7217440269230209, 'citations': ['https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/depression/symptoms-causes/syc-20356007', 'https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2022/22_0003.htm', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Major_depressive_disorder', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666915323001695'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09319381050189027, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation states that physical inactivity leads to increased depressive symptoms via direct neurochemical changes, specifically serotonin depletion.  \\nStep 2: It claims this pathway is immediate and does not require any intermediary variables, and that reductions in physical activity will always and uniformly increase depressive symptoms.  \\nStep 3: This reasoning overlooks the complexity of both depression and physical inactivity. In reality, the relationship is not always direct, immediate, or uniform; multiple factors (psychological, social, genetic, environmental) can mediate or moderate the relationship.  \\nStep 4: The explanation also assumes a one-way, deterministic pathway, ignoring the possibility of bidirectional or non-linear relationships (e.g., depressive symptoms may also cause inactivity).  \\nStep 5: The claim that \\"any reduction\\" in activity will increase symptoms \\"regardless of other factors\\" is logically unsound, as it ignores individual differences and other influences.  \\nStep 6: The exclusion of intermediary variables and the assertion of uniformity oversimplifies the causal process."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.7593078734099353, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5976629831698538, 'motivation': 'Physical inactivity (SBQ) leads to an increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) b