   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2073212754786318, 'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.6839412370037312, 'prob_std': 0.2870899617709407, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09489308322843194, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2023.1234977/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11608784/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosocial_behavior', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10614638/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7109579489362239, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation starts by asserting a direct positive causal relationship between prosocial behavior and perceived social support. It then elaborates that engaging in prosocial acts can lead to greater perceived social support, reasoning that such behavior fosters reciprocal interactions and strengthens social connections. This reasoning is internally coherent: if an individual behaves prosocially, it is plausible that others may reciprocate or that the individual feels more connected, both of which could increase their perception of being supported. The converse (decreased prosocial behavior leading to reduced perceived support) is also logically consistent. The explanation clearly links the source variable to the target variable through a plausible, step-wise social mechanism. There are no logical gaps or unwarranted assumptions within the explanation.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6315338513409094, 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived social support (MSPSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Engaging in prosocial acts such as helping others or cooperating in group settings can lead individuals to perceive greater social support from their environment. This is because such be