   Properties: {'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.896685146391973, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10447879123794572, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Step 1: The explanation asserts a negative causal relationship, where increased prosocial behavior leads to decreased perceived stress. The mechanism proposed is that prosocial actions shift cognitive resources away from personal stressors toward helping others, thus reducing stress perception.  \\nStep 2: The logic suggests a zero-sum allocation of cognitive resources: focusing on others\\u2019 problems leaves less mental capacity for one\\u2019s own, which purportedly reduces stress.  \\nStep 3: It further claims that this shift results in reduced activity in stress-processing brain regions, leading to lower perceived stress.  \\nStep 4: The reverse is also described: less prosocial behavior means more attention to personal stressors, resulting in higher perceived stress.  \\nStep 5: Internally, the steps are coherent: engaging in prosocial acts \\u2192 attention shifted outward \\u2192 reduced focus on self-stress \\u2192 less perceived stress. The explanation maintains logical consistency and describes a plausible psychological mechanism.  \\nStep 6: However, one potential weakness is the assumption that cognitive resources are strictly limited and reallocating attention away from one\\u2019s own problems always leads to less perceived stress. There is no consideration of possible negative effects of prosocial behavior (