201. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[POSITIVE]->Sleep disturbance (PSQI)
   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3765388675827221, 'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) cause sleep disturbance (PSQI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increased IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, directly reduces inflammatory activity, which is known to improve sleep quality and decrease sleep disturbances. Conversely, decreased IL-10 can lead to increased inflammation and worsen sleep disturbance. The causal direction is from anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) to sleep disturbance (PSQI), not the reverse.', 'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.8088029924553133, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.09546105571712565, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation claims that increased anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 would enhance (increase) sleep disturbance, suggesting a positive relationship between anti-inflammatory processes and sleep problems. The reasoning provided is that excessive anti-inflammatory activity could suppress immune activity too much, which is said to be necessary for regular sleep cycles, and that this could disrupt neural and immune balance, leading to worse sleep quality."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5761201628384938, 'motivation': 'Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 may enhance sleep disturbance by overly suppressing immune activity, which is necessary for maintaining regular sleep cycles. Excessive anti-inflammatory signaling could disrupt the balance of neural and immune interactions, leading to poorer sleep quality as measured by the PSQI.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased levels of anti-inflammatory cytokines like IL-10 may enhance sleep disturbance by overly suppressing immune activity, which is necessary for maintaining regular sleep cycles. Excessive anti-inflammatory signaling could disrupt the balance of neural and immune interactions, leading to poorer sleep quality as measured by the PSQI.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.02098271346039395, 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6664976663724975, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0003444681576357515, 'judge_prob_std': 0.30896772601216077}

202. Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10)-[NEGATIVE]->Proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6)
   Properties: {'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7994302515888633e+36, 'citations': ['https://www.kidney-international.org/article/S0085-2538(15)50578-4/fulltext', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4360270/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/immunology/articles/10.3389/fimmu.2023.1161067/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0085253815505784', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4176007/'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10013335843786356, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "INCORRECT", "score": 0.0, "reason": "The explanation describes an inverse association between anti-inflammatory (IL-10) and proinflammatory (TNF-alpha, IL-6) processes. It claims that increases in TNF-alpha and IL-6 stimulate IL-10 production as a compensatory response, and that suppression of proinflammatory cytokines reduces IL-10 levels. This reasoning actually describes a positive causal pathway from proinflammatory cytokines to IL-10 (i.e., increased proinflammatory activity causes increased IL-10), not a negative one. The observed negative association is claimed to be due to this compensatory mechanism, but the logic is inconsistent: if proinflammatory markers drive IL-10 up, then their levels should be positively, not negatively, associated. The explanation conflates compensatory feedback with direct negative causation and does not justify a negative (inverse) causal relationship from anti-inflammatory to proinflammatory processes. The described mechanism does not logically establish that anti-inflammatory processes reduce proinflammatory ones; rather, it attributes changes in anti-inflammatory signaling to changes in proinflammatory signaling, which is not the original relationship type specified.\\nVERDICT: INCORRECT\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "INCORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.8099671236777968, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6487246673575303, 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) are upregulated as a compensatory response to heightened proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6). Therefore, increases in TNF-alpha and IL-6 directly stimulate the production of IL-10, leading to an observed negative association. Conversely, when proinflammatory cytokines are suppressed, the need for anti-inflammatory signaling via IL-10 diminishes, resulting in lower IL-10 levels. This dynamic suggests that fluctuations in proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily drive changes in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10), establishing an inverse relationship.', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'spurious_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) are upregulated as a compensatory response to heightened proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6). Therefore, increases in TNF-alpha and IL-6 directly stimulate the production of IL-10, leading to an observed negative association. Conversely, when proinflammatory cytokines are suppressed, the need for anti-inflammatory signaling via IL-10 diminishes, resulting in lower IL-10 levels. This dynamic suggests that fluctuations in proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) primarily drive changes in anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10), establishing an inverse relationship.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8782004000242873, 'min_prob': 0.2303965774453461, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.7218606991310451, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002254688296549048, 'prob_variance': 0.041050751666123333, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.0006799354315695115, 'judge_prob_std': 0.3164385539687975, 'avg_nll': 0.16945841080856516, 'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.327904607757216, 'original_motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally inhibit proinflammatory processes (TNF-alpha and IL-6) by downregulating their production and activity. Increasing IL-10 directly reduces TNF-alpha and IL-6 levels, while decreasing IL-10 allows proinflammatory cytokines to rise. This inverse causal relationship is well-established in immunology.', 'prob_std': 0.20260985086151, 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'judge_verdict': 'INCORRECT', 'perplexity': 1.184663076829086, 'max_window_entropy': 0.6976963517687167}

