   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.2089959131675911, 'session_id': '98776eb9-ce3d-4802-a93f-020e6d9d8c97', 'judge_perplexity': 3.581972893824381e+26, 'prob_std': 0.22716996882247686, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.11057568476772209, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychiatry/articles/10.3389/fpsyt.2023.1130989/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12024626/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8108565835497068, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "CORRECT", "score": 1.0, "reason": "The explanation posits a negative causal relationship where increased anti-inflammatory processes (specifically IL-10) lead to reduced depressive symptoms. The chain of reasoning is as follows: higher IL-10 increases anti-inflammatory activity, this reduces neuroinflammation, and neuroinflammation is implicated in depression, so less neuroinflammation results in fewer depressive symptoms. The logical structure is clear and follows a plausible biological pathway: modifier (IL-10) \\u2192 intermediate (neuroinflammation) \\u2192 outcome (depressive symptoms). The statement also notes the converse (decreasing IL-10 may increase symptoms), maintaining internal consistency. There are no apparent logical gaps, and the mechanism provided (via neuroinflammation) is coherent and links the source and target variables.\\nVERDICT: CORRECT\\nSCORE: 1.0"}], "aggregate_score": 1.0, "aggregate_verdict": "CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6863215344157533, 'motivation': 'Anti-inflammatory processes (IL-10) causally reduce depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Higher levels of IL-10, an anti-inflammatory cytokine, decrease neuroinflammation, which is implicated in the pathophysiology of depression. Therefore, increasing IL-10 leads to fewer depressive symptoms, while decreasing IL-10 may allow depressive symptoms to increase.', 'aggregate_score': 1.0, 'judge_verdict': 'CORRECT', 'judge_min_prob': 0.0, 'perplexity': 1