   Properties: {'session_id': '87271a15-be98-48bd-9a89-a60ad8c9582e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.7999930281861707, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915202/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10689368/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4136523/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1330720/full'], 'judge_prob_variance': 0.10061065504394057, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a specific causal mechanism (social comparison) linking MSPSS to IDS-SR, which satisfies plausibility and is non-circular and logically coherent. Temporality is implied by the proposed direction, but the explanation does not clearly justify why the causal flow should not be reversed (i.e., why depressive symptoms would not affect perceived social support). The strength of the relationship is not convincingly conveyed; the explanation does not assert that this pathway is substantial, only that it is \\"not fully explained by other pathways.\\" Thus, while the mechanism and coherence are addressed, the justification for direction and importance is weak."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'max_prob_diff': 0.767527183037805, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.6209808145760202, 'motivation': 'Perceived social support (MSPSS) causes changes in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) through a process of social comparison. Individuals with higher perceived social support (MSPSS) are more frequently exposed to the successes and positive experiences of their supportive social networks, which can highlight their own shortcomings and lead to an increase in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). Conversely, those with lower perceived social support (MSPSS) have fewer opportunities for such unfavorable comparisons, resulting in fewer depressive symptoms (IDS