   Properties: {'judge_max_window_entropy': 1.3098978310085103, 'original_motivation': 'Sleep disturbance (PSQI) causes perceived stress (PSS) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Poor sleep quality or disturbances impair emotional regulation, making individuals more susceptible to stress. Conversely, improving sleep quality can directly reduce perceived stress. This relationship does not require mediation by other variables in the provided CLD.', 'session_id': '87271a15-be98-48bd-9a89-a60ad8c9582e', 'judge_perplexity': 1.9890556268218034, 'judge_prob_variance': 0.1140232959620833, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"approach": "correctness", "judge_results": [{"judge_id": 1, "model": "gpt-4.1", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT", "score": 0.5, "reason": "The explanation provides a somewhat plausible mechanism (more wakefulness may allow time for rumination, potentially increasing perceived stress), but this is weak and not well-specified, especially since better sleep is generally expected to reduce\\u2014not increase\\u2014perceived stress. Temporality is partially addressed, as the direction is discussed (sleep disturbance preceding stress), but the argument is convoluted and risks reverse causation. The strength of the effect is not convincingly conveyed; the claim is tentative and does not argue that the relationship is substantial. Internal coherence is moderate, but the reasoning is counterintuitive and not clearly justified, limiting overall consistency."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "PARTIALLY_CORRECT"}', 'judge_max_prob_diff': 0.5898482422816029, 'motivation': 'It is plausible to think that better sleep quality, as indicated by lower PSQI scores (less sleep disturbance), leads to higher perceived stress because individuals who spend less time sleeping may have more time to ruminate on stressors. Therefore, as sleep disturbance decreases, perceived stress could paradoxically increase due to increased wakefulness and time to worry.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'PARTIALLY_CORRECT', 'spurious_motivation': 'It is plausible to think that better sleep quality, as indicated by lower PSQI scores (less sleep disturbance), leads to higher perceived stress because individuals who spend less time sleeping may have more time to ruminate on stressors. Therefore, as sleep disturbance decreases, perceived stress could paradoxically increase due to increased wakefulness and time to worry.', 'judge_min_prob': 0.008960005689903114, 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'judge_mean_token_prob': 0.6617834783399725, 'judge_token_prob_slope': -0.00195673348257701, 'judge_prob_std': 0.33767335690291483}

